mechanism:
Mechanism. By WKB, tunnelling through a barrier of height Delta and width w scales as exp(-c w sqrt(Delta)/hbar_eff), while local classical dynamics crosses at rate exp(-beta Delta). When Delta is large and w small, the tunnelling exponent is much smaller. The quantum resource is amplitude leakage through classically forbidden regions; a classical local walker cannot copy it because it has to climb.
Classical adversaries that can copy the EFFECT:
(i) Hamiltonian replica exchange that softens the terms generating the barrier (soft-core sterics, lambda -> 0 in replicas; the target is unchanged at lambda = 1);
(ii) non-local pivot or crankshaft moves that jump thin barriers;
(iii) SQA/PIMC, which reproduces incoherent tunnelling scaling (Isakov et al. [A64]) and can be exponentially faster than SA on spike landscapes (Crosson-Harrow).
New: a census that classifies learned-energy barriers by origin (steric vs pair-distance term) and shape (Delta, w). Tunnelling keeps room only if thin barriers come from the pair-distance terms themselves, because those cannot be softened without changing pi.

kill_result:
Either kills it:
- the barrier census at 44-120 aa finds thin tall barriers only in steric terms, and soft-core HREX gives polynomial round trips;
- barriers are broad (w sqrt(Delta) >= beta Delta), so tunnelling has no advantage in the exponent.

Either kills it:
- a proof that every local frustration-free parent of |sqrt(pi)> for pair-additive energies has gap <= poly * sqrt(delta*), where delta* is the best local classical chain gap;
- numerics in which SDP-optimised non-stoquastic parents never exceed the FMMC gap by more than a square-root factor on small instances.
